 "notes": "Ministry/department 'lines' and 'departments' figures are sourced from the official Demand for Grants (dgsum.pdf, gross Voted+Charged totals), which for a handful of demands (esp. Demand 40 'Repayment of Debt' ~Rs 95.3 lakh crore, and to a lesser extent Demand 85 Railways and Demand 1 Agriculture) include large gross debt-rollover / recoverable-loan / internal-financing flows that are netted out of the headline 'Total Expenditure' figure in Budget at a Glance -- so summing all 'lines'/'departments' BE figures will NOT equal totals.totalExpenditureBE, by design and consistent with the source documents' own reconciliation notes. A cross-check against sumsbe.pdf (net of receipts/recoveries) shows, e.g., Ministry of Defence BE 2026-27 gross (this file) = Rs 8,35,714.74 cr vs Rs 7,84,678.28 cr net in sumsbe.pdf. Demands 39 (Interest Payments), 40 (Repayment of Debt) and 42 (Transfers to States, a residual/miscellaneous demand distinct from tax devolution and Finance Commission grants which are captured in totals.transfersToStates and stateTransfers[].taxDevolution) are not owned by a single ministry so entityId is null with the raw demand name. Demands 49 (Ministry of Home Affairs) and 51 (Police, i.e. CAPFs/IB funded through MHA) are merged into one in-min-home-affairs line. Demands 65 (Law and Justice), 74 (Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions) and 102 (Youth Affairs and Sports) are single combined Demands for Grants that Parliament votes on ministry-wide -- the Demand for Grants documents do not split them by MINISTRIES.md's constituent department, so they appear only as ministry-level lines, not further split into departments. Demand 19 'Ministry of Defence (Civil)' and Demand 21 'Capital Outlay on Defence Services' are both attributed to in-dept-defence (civilian HQ + capital acquisition); Demand 20 'Defence Services (Revenue)' to in-dept-military-affairs; Demand 22 'Defence Pensions' to in-dept-ex-servicemen-welfare. UT administrations without a canonical ministries-* owner (Demands 52-59: Andaman & Nicobar Islands, Chandigarh, Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu, Ladakh, Lakshadweep, and Transfers to Delhi/Jammu & Kashmir/Puducherry) are referenced as st-<code>-government per SCHEMA.md's ID convention; these are owned by the states-* segments, not defined here. totals.defence (Rs 5,94,585 cr) and totals.subsidies (Rs 4,10,495 cr = Fertiliser 1,70,781 + Food 2,27,629 + Petroleum 12,085) are taken from Budget at a Glance's 'Expenditure of Major Items' functional table, which differs from the Demand-for-Grants-based Ministry of Defence total in 'lines' (that includes defence pensions and non-major-item spending; the two are not directly comparable). 'schemes' combines Statement 4A (Centrally Sponsored Schemes) and Statement 4B (Central Sector Schemes), ranked by BE 2026-27 descending; two pure railway accounting/inventory heads ('Manufacturing Suspense', 'Stores Suspense') were excluded as not citizen-facing schemes. Some top Central Sector 'schemes' are really capital work categories (e.g. Railways 'Road Works' under Demand 86 NHAI/roads, 'Rolling Stock'/'New Lines'/'Track Renewals' under Demand 85 Railways, 'Aircraft and Aero Engines'/'Naval Fleet' under Demand 21 defence capital acquisition) rather than named public programmes, but are included as-is because that is how the official Statement 4B classifies them. stateTransfers[].taxDevolution is exact and sourced from Receipts Budget Annex-4 (all 28 states, sums to Rs 15,26,254.58 cr matching totals within rounding; 16th Finance Commission fixed the states' share at 41% of net proceeds of shareable Central taxes for the 2026-31 award period). stateTransfers[].totalTransfers (devolution + all grants: Finance Commission grants, Centrally Sponsored Schemes, Central Sector Schemes, capital transfers, etc.) is deliberately left null for every state: indiabudget.gov.in does not publish a single state-wise table of the ~Rs 10.94 lakh crore in BE 2026-27 non-devolution transfers (Statement 18 gives only the national aggregate); computing it would require aggregating scheme-wise state-level releases from PFMS/individual ministry accounts, which was out of scope for this pass. This is a known, explicit gap rather than an invented number.",
